WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.228
Tomas: Start with the ending.

2
00:00:01.288 --> 00:00:02.104
Tomas: It saves time.

3
00:00:02.324 --> 00:00:03.520
Meera: It saves the ending.

4
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:04.290
Kalyani: Sit down.

5
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.178
Kalyani: The salt is on the table.

6
00:00:06.398 --> 00:00:07.234
Tomas: Is it taxed?

7
00:00:07.454 --> 00:00:08.685
Kalyani: That's the whole film.

8
00:00:12.685 --> 00:00:12.940
Salt.

9
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.147
The British Empire could tax almost anything;

10
00:00:15.207 --> 00:00:18.989
it chose the one thing the poorest Indian
could gather for free.

11
00:00:19.409 --> 00:00:21.375
Meera: A constitution could not be attacked.

12
00:00:21.435 --> 00:00:23.124
Meera: A tax on salt could.

13
00:00:23.344 --> 00:00:25.608
Tomas: So the Empire went after the kitchen.

14
00:00:26.008 --> 00:00:30.536
A constitution could not be attacked directly;
a tax on salt could.

15
00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:35.703
The Congress gave Gandhi the first act of
civil disobedience, expecting his arrest.

16
00:00:36.063 --> 00:00:39.813
The march was called the White Flowing River,
for the khadi the marchers wore.

17
00:00:40.723 --> 00:00:44.752
Meera: The Salt Act of 1882 gave the Raj a monopoly.

18
00:00:44.972 --> 00:00:46.140
Tomas: A monopoly on salt.

19
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.735
Tomas: Not a tax, a leash.

20
00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:48.861
Kalyani: With a receipt.

21
00:00:49.261 --> 00:00:53.539
The Salt Act of 1882 gave the Raj a monopoly
on collection and manufacture.

22
00:00:53.599 --> 00:00:55.398
Salt was free on the coast.

23
00:00:55.458 --> 00:00:58.247
The law made it a crime to take it.

24
00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:03.248
The tax was small at the till and enormous in
a poor family's kitchen.

25
00:01:03.308 --> 00:01:04.926
By 1930 it was 8.

26
00:01:04.986 --> 00:01:07.301
2 percent of British India's tax revenue.

27
00:01:07.361 --> 00:01:10.635
Even Nehru was sceptical when Gandhi chose the
salt tax.

28
00:01:11.961 --> 00:01:15.444
The Act limited all handling of salt to
government depots.

29
00:01:16.561 --> 00:01:19.255
Gandhi called the salt campaign a poor man's
struggle.

30
00:01:21.221 --> 00:01:21.865
Meera: Eleven demands.

31
00:01:21.925 --> 00:01:23.334
Meera: The first is salt.

32
00:01:23.554 --> 00:01:24.841
Tomas: He wrote to the Viceroy.

33
00:01:24.901 --> 00:01:26.248
Tomas: Not a petition, a list.

34
00:01:26.468 --> 00:01:28.558
Kalyani: A list is a polite ultimatum.

35
00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:33.825
On 2 March 1930, Gandhi wrote to the Viceroy
with eleven demands.

36
00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:35.467
Abolish the salt tax.

37
00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:36.699
Cut military spending.

38
00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:37.991
Protect Indian cloth.

39
00:01:38.351 --> 00:01:39.690
Lord Irwin was not impressed.

40
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.947
He wrote to London that a salt campaign did
not keep him awake at night.

41
00:01:44.307 --> 00:01:49.601
The demands included land revenue relief,
military cuts and a tariff on foreign cloth.

42
00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:53.165
Gandhi briefed the world's press before he
left the ashram.

43
00:01:54.561 --> 00:01:57.789
Irwin ignored the letter and refused to meet
him before the march.

44
00:01:59.221 --> 00:02:00.339
Meera: Twelve March.

45
00:02:00.399 --> 00:02:01.578
Meera: Seventy-eight volunteers.

46
00:02:01.798 --> 00:02:02.055
Tomas: Seventy-eight.

47
00:02:02.115 --> 00:02:03.702
Tomas: That is not an army.

48
00:02:03.922 --> 00:02:05.431
Kalyani: It is a congregation.

49
00:02:05.831 --> 00:02:10.949
On 12 March 1930, Gandhi left Sabarmati with
78 volunteers.

50
00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:16.187
They walked for 24 days, 387 kilometres,
through 48 villages.

51
00:02:16.547 --> 00:02:17.471
The line grew.

52
00:02:17.531 --> 00:02:18.784
Farmers, weavers, students, women.

53
00:02:18.844 --> 00:02:22.782
The world's press came to watch a man walk
toward the sea.

54
00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:26.927
The seventy-eight marchers came from almost
every region, caste and creed.

55
00:02:27.742 --> 00:02:30.377
Most of them were between twenty and thirty
years old.

56
00:02:32.342 --> 00:02:35.523
Villages met them with drums and cymbals as
they passed.

57
00:02:36.942 --> 00:02:40.738
They asked the villages for food and water to
wash with, nothing more.

58
00:02:41.542 --> 00:02:44.630
The column stretched at least three kilometres
behind him.

59
00:02:46.202 --> 00:02:47.540
Meera: Six April, half past eight.

60
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.557
Meera: He picks up a lump of salt.

61
00:02:49.777 --> 00:02:50.525
Tomas: One handful.

62
00:02:50.585 --> 00:02:51.797
Tomas: Against an empire.

63
00:02:52.197 --> 00:02:56.713
At 8:30 on the morning of 6 April, Gandhi bent
down and picked up a lump of salt.

64
00:02:57.073 --> 00:02:58.576
The law was broken.

65
00:02:58.636 --> 00:03:00.140
Within weeks, millions joined.

66
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.763
Over 60,000 were jailed.

67
00:03:02.123 --> 00:03:06.210
He raised a lump of salty mud and said he was
shaking the Empire's foundations.

68
00:03:06.723 --> 00:03:10.717
He boiled seawater into illegal salt and told
his followers to do the same.

69
00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:15.386
About seven hundred telegrams went out from
the post office at Jalalpur.

70
00:03:15.923 --> 00:03:19.197
Sarojini Naidu saluted him at Dandi as a law
breaker.

71
00:03:20.583 --> 00:03:22.429
Meera: A retired judge led the raid.

72
00:03:22.649 --> 00:03:24.518
Tomas: A judge, leading the lathis.

73
00:03:24.738 --> 00:03:26.282
Meera: The law protected the monopoly.

74
00:03:26.502 --> 00:03:27.826
Tomas: Then the law is the weapon.

75
00:03:28.046 --> 00:03:29.764
Kalyani: The refusal is the evidence.

76
00:03:29.984 --> 00:03:31.493
Tomas: The lathis are the reply.

77
00:03:31.713 --> 00:03:32.677
Kalyani: Say it loud.

78
00:03:33.077 --> 00:03:36.920
Gandhi was arrested in a mango grove before
the Dharasana raid began.

79
00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.955
The Raj answered with lathis.

80
00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:43.526
At Dharasana, volunteers walked into the clubs
and did not raise a hand.

81
00:03:43.886 --> 00:03:47.248
The world saw the choice the Empire made.

82
00:03:47.308 --> 00:03:49.447
The story reached 1,350 newspapers.

83
00:03:49.807 --> 00:03:53.650
A retired judge of seventy-six led the raid
with Kasturba beside him.

84
00:03:54.407 --> 00:03:58.076
Both were arrested, and Sarojini Naidu took
command of the march.

85
00:03:59.007 --> 00:04:02.130
She told them they would be beaten but must
not raise a hand.

86
00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:04.849
Meera: March 1931.

87
00:04:04.909 --> 00:04:06.152
Meera: A pact.

88
00:04:06.372 --> 00:04:07.717
Tomas: Ninety thousand prisoners out.

89
00:04:07.777 --> 00:04:09.182
Tomas: The salt tax stays.

90
00:04:09.402 --> 00:04:10.900
Kalyani: A pause, not a verdict.

91
00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:15.322
In March 1931, Gandhi and Irwin signed a pact.

92
00:04:15.382 --> 00:04:18.103
Over 90,000 political prisoners were released.

93
00:04:18.463 --> 00:04:20.702
The Raj did not abolish the salt tax.

94
00:04:20.762 --> 00:04:22.426
The movement did not win everything.

95
00:04:22.486 --> 00:04:26.509
It won something harder to take away: a nation
had seen its own power.

96
00:04:26.869 --> 00:04:30.816
Gandhi was released in 1931 to meet Irwin on
equal terms.

97
00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:35.695
Congress ended satyagraha as official policy
in 1934.

98
00:04:36.129 --> 00:04:36.366
Tomas: Armies.

99
00:04:36.426 --> 00:04:38.799
Tomas: And no answer to a handful of salt.

100
00:04:39.019 --> 00:04:40.145
Kalyani: It was a refusal.

101
00:04:40.545 --> 00:04:41.606
The Empire had armies.

102
00:04:41.666 --> 00:04:44.469
It had no answer to a man picking up salt.

103
00:04:44.829 --> 00:04:48.672
Time magazine named Gandhi its Man of the Year
for 1930.

104
00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:51.103
Tomas: So consent is the whole machine?

105
00:04:51.323 --> 00:04:53.308
Meera: And it runs on people saying yes.

106
00:04:53.528 --> 00:04:54.513
Kalyani: Then say no.

107
00:04:54.573 --> 00:04:55.618
Kalyani: Pass the salt.

108
00:04:56.018 --> 00:05:00.569
A provincial governor conceded that England
could hold India only by consent.
